* 20 Oct 26 dm Request 29 (vb.open.sdnet) asks the OS switch of the account it names for a VOC pointer that leads out of the account: an F-pointer outside the account's directory
*           is refused (error ELSE, status 3035, audit 'api refused request=29 reason=os gate off ...') unless that account holds os-on (or SDSYS in its own account).  It
*           opened any such file for a caller the NET gate admitted.  Proved on a real `sd -Q` by sandbox-req29.py section X.
* 19 Oct 26 dm S.65 follow-ups (K1, K3, K8a).  (1) The message of an ON ERROR reply is emptied at the top of every request, so an ON ERROR clause that had nothing to say no
*           longer sends the text of the last refusal (measured: a WRITE with an over-long id answered 'Invalid call name'); the SDNet client now shows an ON ERROR reply's
*           text.  Message 11521 was shortened to 98 characters, under the 100 the client shows.  (2) The refusal of a login that is in neither sdapi nor sdfiles is audited
*           with the reason 'not a member of sdapi or sdfiles' (it named sdapi only, but the decision is made on both).  (3) vb.filelock and vb.fileunlock used
*           files(i), the scratch variable of the last open, instead of files(fno): a lock or an unlock went to whichever file had been opened last (as vb.clearfile's
*           did until 17 Oct).  Proved on a real `sd -Q` by sandbox-req29.py sections F and G.
* 18 Oct 26 dm S.65 write group: a files-only login may change state or take a lock only if the user is ALSO in the Linux group sdfileswrite (off for a
*           new account; SDSYS gives it with modify.account files-write-on).  vb.scram.final reads it (three-valued, like sdfiles) into files.write
*           (common block, set with files.only, never cleared); !apifilesok takes it as a third argument; a request refused for want of it is
*           audited with write=off and answered with 11521.  The login line gains write=on|off.  sdapi members are untouched.  vb.readv asks for itself
*           (request 41 is in the table for a plain READV; its flags 1, 2, 5 and 6 take a lock, refused the same way).  vb.open opens
*           "dict <file>" in any case as a DICTIONARY (open 'DICT', name): the SDNet handler sends it in lower case, which _voc_ref (upper case
*           DICT only) did not understand, so a remote dictionary was never opened.
* 18 Oct 26 dm S.66: the shared-account gates (K$SHARED.READ / .WRITE) are read from ACC$SHARED.READ / .WRITE and loaded at every entry to an account,
*           as NET is (read ON unless the record says no; both OFF for a path or an unreadable register); the sdsys pair gets both ON.  The
*           refusal of a write to a guarded file takes its text from !edit_msg, which knows the kinds the kernel can now report.
* 17 Oct 26 dm S.65 slice 2c: a second API route, files only.  A user in sdusers and sdfiles but not in sdapi logs in (vb.scram.final)
*           and the session is marked (files.only, in the common block, set once and never cleared); sdapi wins when both.  The
*           request loop asks !apifilesok before the dispatch and refuses, with 11480, anything it does not allow.  The SDNet login.
* 17 Oct 26 dm S.65 slice 1: the NET gate (K$NET; SDNet remote files, does nothing yet) is loaded at every entry to an account, as SD.ACCOUNTS is,
*           from ACC$NET; the OS user sdsys entering the sdsys account has it ON (the administrator passes).
* 17 Oct 26 dm S.64 part 2: an API session of SDSYS that enters the sdsys account loads the three edit gates ON.  An API session carries
*           no administrator flag, and the sdsys register record has no edit fields (modify.account refuses sdsys), so SDSYS over the
*           local door could not compile or save to a VOC, a dictionary or a program file.  Only for the OS user sdsys entering the sdsys
*           account, the pair vb.account already exempts from the group test; every other account loads what its own record says.
*           AND THE API SERVER ASKS THE GATES FOR ITS CLIENT: this program is $internal, so the kernel's edit test never saw its WRITE,
*           WRITEU, DELETE, DELETEU, CLEARFILE or CREATING.SEQKEY write of a file the client opened (the plan's "refused by the same kernel
*           test" was not so).  check.edit asks before each; an OPENSEQ of a VOC, a dictionary or a program file the session may not change
*           is opened read only.  vb.clearfile cleared files(i), a stale variable, instead of files(fno).
* 15 Oct 26 dm QM4 gaps Q-8: INHIBIT.LOGIN.  An API session is refused (11281) while the administrator has logins
*           inhibited, after the backup login hold and before any credential is looked at.
* 12 Oct 26 dm Call names: the lower of 128 and MAXIDLEN (openQM item 241), 63 at the default.  Refused logins
*           wait PWDELAY seconds (item 106), 3 by default.
* 11 Oct 26 dm A record of a data collection file is a collection when it is read, which
*           the response cannot hold: it goes to the client as its JSON text.
*           COMPILED ONLY - the API server was not run against a collection file.
* 06 Oct 26 dm PAL-17 (parity audit): the two refusals to enter an account (suspended;
*           not in the account's group) are on the audit trail, as the Windows
*           port's ACCOUNT REFUSED lines (branch=2 and branch=4).
* 04 Oct 26 dm S.60: a refused SCRAM login is audited BEFORE the three-second
*           failed-credential delay, not after it (scram.bad.cred,
*           exit.vb.scram.fail).  A client that dropped during the delay used to
*           leave no audit line.
* 01 Oct 26 dm S.50: the login hold.  vb.scram.first and vb.local.login refuse
*           a new API session (13002) while BACKUP.ACCOUNT or RESTORE.ACCOUNT
*           runs, as LOGIN does.
* 20 Sep 26 dm S.29 part 3: vb.scram's sdapi gate is BACK (10073, the port's
*           number and text).  The owner's ruling of 18/19 Sep reverses S.28's
*           disposal of the per-account route: membership of sdapi IS the API
*           route, MODIFY.ACCOUNT turns it off and on, and this is the test that
*           makes the word mean something.  status() is read so "could not tell"
*           refuses with its own audit reason rather than reading as "not a
*           member"; sdsys is exempt by name, as at vb.account.
* 19 Sep 26 dm SDSYS's API door asks the KERNEL who opened the socket, not the
*           address (owner, 19 Sep 2026: only a user logged in as sdsys reaches
*           SDSYS, and local API access is worth a second password).  An
*           address cannot see through an ssh tunnel and sdsys can never own
*           one, so !peer_local alone admitted a tunnel opened by anybody with
*           a login here.  system(43) is the socket peer's OS user
*           (SO_PEERCRED); 10922 refuses when it is not sdsys, 10174 keeps the
*           remote case.  SDSYS over the API is now Unix-socket only.
* 18 Sep 26 dm TEARDOWN (owner's 18 Sep 2026 decision).  vb.account's
*           tier-ordering gate is gone with the tier model (S.25); vb.scram's
*           sdapi test is gone - the API is open to every account (S.28) - and
*           the remote-administrator gate is now SDSYS's own door: refused
*           unless the connection is from this machine (10174).  The suspended
*           test reads ACC$SUSPENDED (W.7).
* 13 Sep 26 dm  Account names are lower case (plan M, owner): the register is
*               read with the name downcased.
* 13 Sep 26 dm  An account's VOC file is voc on disk (plan M3 D3).
* 13 Sep 26 dm  Opens sdsys $ipc and accounts by their lower-case names on disk
*               (plan M3 D1).
* 12 Sep 26 dm  The LOGIN paragraph read folds as typed, then lower (plan M1).
* 19 Jan 04  0.6.1 SD launch. Earlier history details suppressed.
* 20240219x mab rebrand as APISRVR
*               always run in secure mode
* 28 Jul 24 mab remove enter.package / exit.package (commands not supported)
* rev 0.9.0 Jan 25 mab test for user member of account on logto
* 11 Sep 26 dm  vb.account refuses a suspended account with 10003, the same
*               text as every other refusal (PORT_ADOPTION 12, the port's).
* 11 Sep 26 dm  vb.login writes an API REFUSED audit record at every refusal;
*               a name reaches the record only after valid_os_name passed it
*               (PORT_ADOPTION 13, the port's).
* 14 Sep 26 dm  vb.account applies the tier ordering (!tier_allows, 10003), the
*               port's of 29 Aug 2026; an account name folds to lower case, so
*               WHO names it as it is on disk (W.4).
* 14 Sep 26 dm  vb.login takes a password of any length and refuses an empty
*               one, as the port does (S.14).
* 14 Sep 26 dm  SCRAM-SHA-256 login, requests 47 and 48 (vb.scram.first,
*               vb.scram.final), from the Windows port's APISRVR of 19-23 Aug
*               2026 (W.4 SCRAM phase 3).  Additive: request 24 still works
*               until phase 5.  Linux differences, each marked where it sits:
*               $cred ids are lower case; the API-use test is sdusers (this
*               tree has no sdapi); the port's remote-administrator gate is not
*               here (no !peer_local); becoming the user is initgroups/setuid.
* 14 Sep 26 dm  Request 24, the cleartext login, is retired: vb.login answers
*               5275 and drops the connection, the port's (W.4 SCRAM phase 5).
* 14 Sep 26 dm  vb.scram.final refuses an administrator (tier or sdadmin) whose
*               connection is not from this machine, with 10174 - the port's
*               remote-administrator gate (S.17).
* 14 Sep 26 dm  vb.scram.final requires sdapi membership (10073), the port's
*               per-account API route (S.16).
