* ======================================================================
* account.entry.check  -  may this user enter this account?  ONE PLACE FOR THE RULE.
* In:  ent.name       the account name, lower case, as asked for
*      ent.rec        its register record
*      ent.suspended  true if the record says SUSPENDED
* Out: ent.refused    true if the user may not enter it: the refusal is on the audit trail and server.error, error.msg and
*                     abort.message are set (the text is 10003 for every reason, so the caller cannot tell "no such account" from
*                     "not a member" from "suspended")
*
* 18 Oct 26 dm - REFACTOR, BEHAVIOUR PRESERVED.  These two tests were written out in vb.account (SrvrAccount, request 3) and nowhere
*   else, so request 29 (open an 'account;file' file) did not ask them at all and opened the file of an account the caller could not
*   LOGTO.  Both requests call this now.  The text of the audit lines and of the refusal is as it was.

account.entry.check:
ent.refused = @false

* 11 Sep 26 dm - A SUSPENDED ACCOUNT IS NOT ENTERED (PORT_ADOPTION 12; the port's APISRVR of 27 Aug 2026).  The third door, after LOGIN and
*   CPROC's LOGTO; an API caller reaches an account through neither.
*
*   IT REUSES 10003, AND THAT IS THE RULE HERE.  Every refusal in this routine gives the same text, so a caller cannot tell "no such
*   account" from "not a member" from "suspended", and the API does not enumerate the register for anyone probing it.  No administrator
*   exemption: an API session carries none.
if ent.suspended then
* 06 Oct 26 dm - PAL-17 (parity audit): the refusal is on the audit trail, in the Windows port's words (its ACCOUNT REFUSED branch=2),
*   with the account asked for appended.  The CALLER still sees only 10003.
   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'account refused branch=2 suspended account=' : ent.name)  ;* 07 Oct 26 PAL-24: the audit trail is lower case
   server.error = SV$ERROR
   error.msg = sysmsg(10003) ;* User not allowed in requested account
   abort.message = error.msg
   ent.refused = @true
   return
end

* rev 0.9.0 * is user member of account?
if not(is_grp_member(@logname, ent.rec<ACC$GROUP>)) then
* 18 Sep 26 dm - TEARDOWN (S.26).  SDSYS ENTERS ITS OWN ACCOUNT WITHOUT A GROUP.  The SDSYS register record's ACC$GROUP reads "sdsys",
*   which is no Linux group - the OS user IS the account, so there is nothing to be a member of.  The local API door above (10174)
*   already confined it to this machine; the exemption is by NAME and for the sdsys account only.
   if not(downcase(@logname) = 'sdsys' and ent.name = 'sdsys') then
* 06 Oct 26 dm - PAL-17: audited as the Windows port's branch=4 not.in.group, with the account asked for appended.
      void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'account refused branch=4 not.in.group user=' : @logname : ' group=' : ent.rec<ACC$GROUP> : ' account=' : ent.name)
      server.error = SV$ERROR
      error.msg = sysmsg(10003) ;* User not allowed in requested account
      abort.message = error.msg
      ent.refused = @true
   end
end
return

* ======================================================================
* sdnet.account.check  -  request 29 (open an 'account;file' file): may THIS caller use THIS account's file?
* In:  net.acct       the account named in the request, as typed
* Out: net.refused    true if not allowed: server.error / status say so (set here)
*      net.acc.path   the account's directory, from its register record, when allowed
*
* 18 Oct 26 dm - The request used to ask only bit 2 of NETFILES.  With the bit set, ANY API login could open the VOC-named file of ANY
*   account (sandbox-req29.py reads it back from the disk: an account the caller is not a member of, a suspended one, one named by a
*   path, one whose NET gate is off, and under SDCLIENT=1).  The caller must now be allowed to enter the account by the rule request 3
*   applies (account.entry.check), the account must have a register record (a path names none, so nothing says who may enter it),
*   and its NET gate must be on - the administrator's own account excepted, as at SrvrAccount.

sdnet.account.check:
net.refused = @true
net.acc.path = ''
net.acct = downcase(net.acct)
net.rec = ''
net.found = @false
if net.acct # '' and index(net.acct, @ds, 1) = 0 then
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to net.f then
      read net.rec from net.f, net.acct then net.found = @true
      close net.f
   end
end
if not(net.found) then
   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'api refused request=29 reason=no register record user=' : @logname : ' account=' : net.acct)
   set.status ER$XREMOTE
   server.error = SV$ELSE
   return
end

ent.name = net.acct
ent.rec = net.rec
ent.suspended = (upcase(trim(net.rec<ACC$SUSPENDED>)) = 'SUSPENDED')
gosub account.entry.check
if ent.refused then return

if not(upcase(trim(net.rec<ACC$NET>)) = 'YES' or (downcase(@logname) = 'sdsys' and net.acct = 'sdsys')) then
   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'api refused request=29 reason=net gate off user=' : @logname : ' account=' : net.acct)
   set.status ER$XREMOTE
   server.error = SV$ELSE
   return
end

net.acc.path = parse.pathname.tokens(net.rec<ACC$PATH>)
net.refused = @false
return

* ======================================================================
* SrvrAccount  -  Logto named account
* In:  account name or path

vb.account:
* Run the ON.LOGTO paragraph, if it exists

readv i from @voc,'ON.LOGTO',0 on error null
then execute 'ON.LOGTO' trapping aborts capturing response

* 14 Sep 26 dm - LOWER CASE, GOING PAST THE PORT, which upcases (plan M: no
*   account name exists in two casings).  WHO is set from this at the end, so
*   an upcase here showed ZZREL1 over the API where CPROC's LOGTO shows zzrel1.
*   A PATH is left as typed - a Linux path is case-sensitive, and upcasing one
*   could only ever have missed it.
new.account = cmnd
if index(new.account, @ds, 1) = 0 then new.account = downcase(new.account)

old.account.path = account.path

if len(new.account) < 1 or len(new.account) > MAX.ACCOUNT.NAME.LEN then
   server.error = SV$ERROR
   error.msg = sysmsg(5037) ;* Invalid account name
   goto revert.to.old.account
end

account.path = new.account
acc.suspended = @false
* 13 Oct 26 dm - S.63 (owner, second sitting: "api should read switches").  The account's SH and OS.EXECUTE
*   switches are read here from its register record, as LOGIN reads them at sign-on and CPROC's LOGTO
*   reloads them, and are set only once the account has been entered (below), so a refusal or a revert
*   leaves the previous account's switches with the previous account.  FAIL CLOSED: both start off for
*   every call, and an account named by a path, or with no record, keeps them off.  Before this an API
*   session never held either switch, so an account given sh-on or os-on had neither over the API.
api.sh.grant = @false
api.os.grant = @false
api.sd.grant = @false      ;* 16 Oct 26: the SD.ACCOUNTS gate
api.shared.read.grant = @false    ;* 18 Oct 26, S.66: the shared-account gates; account-scoped, so loaded at every entry.  FAIL CLOSED: a name given as a path
api.shared.write.grant = @false   ;*   or an unreadable register leaves both off (read is ON only when a record exists and does not say 'no')
api.net.grant = @false     ;* 17 Oct 26, S.65 slice 1: the NET gate (SDNet remote files; does nothing yet); account-scoped, so loaded at every entry
api.voc.grant = @false     ;* 17 Oct 26, S.64: the edit gates; loaded at the FIRST entry only (below)
api.bp.grant = @false
api.dict.grant = @false
if index(new.account, @ds, 1) = 0 then
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to acc.f then
      read acc.rec from acc.f, downcase(new.account) then
         account.path = parse.pathname.tokens(acc.rec<ACC$PATH>)
         acc.suspended = (upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$SUSPENDED>)) = 'SUSPENDED')
         api.sh.grant = (upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$SH>)) = 'YES')
         api.os.grant = (upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$OS.EXEC>)) = 'YES')
         api.sd.grant = (upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$SD.ACCOUNTS>)) = 'YES')
         api.net.grant = (upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$NET>)) = 'YES')
         api.shared.read.grant = (upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$SHARED.READ>)) # 'NO')     ;* 18 Oct 26, S.66: blank = ON
         api.shared.write.grant = (upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$SHARED.WRITE>)) = 'YES')
         api.voc.grant = (upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$VOC.EDIT>)) = 'YES')
         api.bp.grant = (upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$BP.EDIT>)) = 'YES')
         api.dict.grant = (upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$DICT.EDIT>)) = 'YES')
      end
      close acc.f
   end
end

* 17 Oct 26 dm - S.64 part 2: SDSYS is the administrator everywhere else (USR_ADMIN passes every edit test), but an API session carries no
*   such flag and the sdsys register record holds no edit fields (modify.account refuses sdsys), so the record above leaves all three off
*   and SDSYS over the local door would be refused at its first save to a VOC, a dictionary or a program file and at BASIC / CATALOG.
*   So the sdsys account entered by the OS user sdsys - the pair vb.account's group test already exempts, and the only user the door
*   (10174 / 10922, in the SCRAM handler) admits as sdsys: a process running as sdsys, on the Unix socket - has all three on.  These
*   are variables only: they are loaded into the kernel with the other gates below, at the first entry to an account and never again, and
*   only after every refusal in this routine has passed.  No other user and no other account gets more than its own record gives.
if downcase(@logname) = 'sdsys' and downcase(new.account) = 'sdsys' then
   api.voc.grant = @true
   api.bp.grant = @true
   api.dict.grant = @true
   api.shared.read.grant = @true    ;* 18 Oct 26, S.66: an API session of sdsys follows shared and can write its records (it has no administrator flag, so no structure change)
   api.shared.write.grant = @true
   api.net.grant = @true    ;* 17 Oct 26, S.65 slice 1: the administrator passes the NET gate too (it does nothing yet)
end

* 18 Oct 26 dm - THE RULE FOR ENTERING AN ACCOUNT (a suspended account is not entered; the user must be a member of its group; every refusal
*   is 10003 and audited) lives in account.entry.check, above, which request 29 asks as well.  Its comments are the old ones of this place.
ent.name = new.account
ent.rec = acc.rec
ent.suspended = acc.suspended
gosub account.entry.check
if ent.refused then goto revert.to.old.account

* rev 0.9.0 * is user member of account?   
* (done in account.entry.check, above)

* 18 Sep 26 dm - TEARDOWN (S.25).  The tier-ordering gate is gone with the
*   tier model: a grant is Linux group membership, and the group test above is
*   the whole of the wall.

if not(ospath(account.path, OS$CD)) then
   server.error = SV$ERROR
   error.msg = sysmsg(5019, account.path) ;* Unable to change to directory %1
   goto revert.to.old.account
end

openpath "voc" to new.voc else
   server.error = SV$ERROR
   error.msg = sysmsg(5163) ;* Directory is not a SD account
   goto revert.to.old.account
end

i = ospath("", os$flush.cache)

account.path = ospath("", os$cwd)     ;* Ensure @PATH is absolute path
initial.account.path = account.path   ;* Make WHO show only one element
who = new.account
void kernel(K$SET.ACCOUNT, who)   ;* 14 Oct 26 - the user table's account (LISTU, sheet 24 and 29)

voc = new.voc

* 0452 Set up the private catalogue pathname

private.catalogue = 'cat'
read voc.rec from voc, "$PRIVATE.CATALOGUE" then
   if upcase(voc.rec[1,1]) = 'X' then private.catalogue = voc.rec<2>
end

i = kernel(K$PRIVATE.CATALOGUE, private.catalogue)

* 13 Oct 26 dm - S.63: every way out of this routine that keeps the new account has passed; load its
*   switches now (see where they were read, above).  The administrator flag is not touched: an API session
*   carries none, so SDSYS over the local socket is held to its own switches like anybody else.
void kernel(K$SH,      api.sh.grant)
void kernel(K$OS.EXEC, api.os.grant)
void kernel(K$SD.ACCOUNTS, api.sd.grant)
void kernel(K$SHARED.READ,  api.shared.read.grant)    ;* 18 Oct 26, S.66: account-scoped like NET, so every entry and every SDLogto loads them
void kernel(K$SHARED.WRITE, api.shared.write.grant)
void kernel(K$NET, api.net.grant)  ;* 17 Oct 26, S.65 slice 1: account-scoped like SD.ACCOUNTS, so every entry and every SDLogto loads it

* 17 Oct 26 dm - S.64: the edit gates are the SIGN-ON account's (owner, 17 Oct), so they are loaded at the first entry to an account
*   and never again; an SDLogto leaves them as they were.  master.login.done is set just below, once, at that same first entry.
if not(master.login.done) then
   void kernel(K$VOC.EDIT,  api.voc.grant)
   void kernel(K$BP.EDIT,   api.bp.grant)
   void kernel(K$DICT.EDIT, api.dict.grant)
end

* 15 Oct 26 dm - sheet 170: the MASTER.LOGIN paragraph of the SDSYS VOC is run for an API session as CPROC runs it for an
*   interactive or phantom one: once, when the session first enters an account, before that account's own LOGIN.  (Entering
*   another account with LOGTO runs the new account's LOGIN only, as it does for a terminal session.)  There is no such
*   paragraph unless the administrator wrote one.  It runs in the account just entered, under that account's switches.
if not(master.login.done) then
   master.login.done = @true
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'voc' to master.f on error null
   then
      read master.rec from master.f, 'MASTER.LOGIN' on error null
      then
         if upcase(master.rec[1,2]) = 'PA' then
            execute field(master.rec, @fm, 2, 9999999) trapping aborts capturing master.out
         end
      end
      close master.f
   end
end

* Run the LOGIN paragraph, if it exists

* 18 Aug 26 Windows port - AS TYPED, THEN LOWER, port PROJECT_STATUS 5.12 (b).
*   @voc is a DYNAMIC file, so this is an exact byte match; without the second
*   attempt an API session would silently stop running the LOGIN paragraph the
*   moment the VOC id moves.  Blocked THEN/ELSE deliberately - see 5.12 trap 3.
readv i from @voc, 'LOGIN', 0 on error null
then
   execute 'LOGIN' trapping aborts capturing response
end else
   readv i from @voc, 'login', 0 on error null
   then
      execute 'login' trapping aborts capturing response
   end
end

return

revert.to.old.account:
if not(ospath(old.account.path, OS$CD)) then
   server.error = SV$ERROR
   error.msg = sysmsg(5162) ;* Fatal error : Cannot return to previous account
   return to abort.vbsrvr
end

return

* ======================================================================
* Open file
* In:  fname
* Out: fileno (short integer)

vb.open:
if kernel(K$SDNET, -1)  or config("SDCLIENT") # 0 then
   abort.message = sysmsg(5262) ;* File access is disabled
   server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
   return
end

* Locate a spare file table entry

n = inmat(files)

for i = 1 to n
   if not(fileinfo(files(i), FL$OPEN)) then goto vb.open.continue
next i

* Extend file table

dim files(n + 50)
i = n + 1

vb.open.continue:
* Open file to file table entry i

* 18 Oct 26 dm - S.65: "dict <file>" in ANY case is a dictionary, opened as OPEN 'DICT', <file>.  The single-argument OPEN looks the VOC for a
*   file named "dict <file>", which _voc_ref understands only with an upper case DICT; the SDNet handler (and netfile) send it in lower case, so a
*   remote dictionary was never opened (ELSE 3007) and COUNT, LIST, SORT and SELECT on a remote file stopped at "Cannot open dictionary".  Only
*   the word and the one space are taken off: the name goes to the same OPEN, so every refusal that applies to a file applies to the dictionary.
if upcase(cmnd[1,5]) = 'DICT ' then
   open 'DICT', trim(cmnd[6,9999]) to files(i)
   on error server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
   then
      if bitand(fileinfo(files(i), FL$FLAGS), FL$FLAGS.TRUSTED) then
         server.error = SV$ELSE
      end else
         response = iconv(i, 'ISL')
      end
   end
   else server.error = SV$ELSE
end else
   open cmnd to files(i)
   on error server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
   then
      if bitand(fileinfo(files(i), FL$FLAGS), FL$FLAGS.TRUSTED) then
         server.error = SV$ELSE
      end else
         response = iconv(i, 'ISL')
      end
   end
   else server.error = SV$ELSE
end

      return

      * ======================================================================
      * Close file
      * In:  fileno (short integer)
      * Out:

vb.close:
      fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
      if fno > SEQ.BASE then            ;* a sequential file (sheet 441)
         gosub check.seq
         if err then return
         closeseq seqfiles(si)
         seq.state(si) = 0
         return
      end
      gosub check.file
      if err then return

      close files(fno)   ;* 0346

      return

      * ======================================================================
      * Read record
      * In:  fileno (short integer)
      *      id
      * Out: record

vb.read:
      fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
      gosub check.file
      if err then return

      id = cmnd[3,99]
      read rec from files(fno), id
      on error
      server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
   end then
      response = rec
   end else
      server.error = SV$ELSE
      end

      return

      * ======================================================================
      * Read record with shared lock
      * In:  fileno (short integer)
      *      id
      * Out: record

vb.readl:
      fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
      gosub check.file
      if err then return

      id = cmnd[3,99]
      readl rec from files(fno), id
      on error
      server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
   end locked
      server.error = SV$LOCKED
      end then
         response = rec
      end else
         server.error = SV$ELSE
         end

         return

         * ======================================================================
         * Read record with shared lock, waiting if locked
         * In:  fileno (short integer)
         *      id
         * Out: record

vb.readlw:
         fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
         gosub check.file
         if err then return

         id = cmnd[3,99]
         readl rec from files(fno), id
         on error
         server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
      end then
         response = rec
      end else
         server.error = SV$ELSE
         end

         return

         * ======================================================================
         * Read record with exclusive lock
         * In:  fileno (short integer)
         *      id
         * Out: record

vb.readu:
         fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
         gosub check.file
         if err then return

         id = cmnd[3,99]
         readu rec from files(fno), id
         on error
         server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
      end locked
         server.error = SV$LOCKED
         end then
            response = rec
         end else
            server.error = SV$ELSE
            end

            return

            * ======================================================================
            * Read record with exclusive lock, waiting if locked
            * In:  fileno (short integer)
            *      id
            * Out: record

vb.readuw:
            fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
            gosub check.file
            if err then return

            id = cmnd[3,99]
            readu rec from files(fno), id
            on error
            server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
         end then
            response = rec
         end else
            server.error = SV$ELSE
            end

            return

            * ======================================================================
            * Select file
            * In:  file no  (short int)
            *      list no  (short int)

vb.select:
            fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
            gosub check.file
            if err then return

            listno = oconv(cmnd[3,2], 'ISL')
            gosub check.list
            if err then return

            select files(fno) to listno

            return

            * ======================================================================
            * Read next entry from select list
            * In:  listno (short integer)
            * Out: id

vb.readnext:
            listno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
            gosub check.list
            if err then return

            readnext response from listno else server.error = SV$ELSE

               return

               * ======================================================================
               * Clear select list
               * In:  listno (short integer)

vb.clearselect:
               listno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
               gosub check.list
               if err then return

               clearselect listno

               return

               * ======================================================================
               * Read select list
               * In:  listno (short integer)

vb.readlist:
               listno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
               gosub check.list
               if err then return

               readlist response from listno else response = ''

               return

               * ======================================================================
               * Release lock
               * In:  fileno (short integer)
               *      id

vb.release:
               fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
               if fno then               ;* 0249
                  gosub check.file
                  if err then return
               end

               id = cmnd[3,99]

               begin case
                  case fno = 0
                     release

                  case id = ''
                     release files(fno)

                  case 1
                     release files(fno), id
               end case

               return

               * ======================================================================
               * Write record
               * In:  fileno (short integer)
               *      id_len (short integer)
               *      id
               *      data

vb.write:
               fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
               gosub check.file
               if err then return

               id.len = oconv(cmnd[3,2], 'ISL')
               id = cmnd[5,id.len]

               gosub check.edit   ;* 17 Oct 26 dm, S.64 part 2: this program is $internal, so the kernel does not ask the edit gates; check.edit does
               if err then return

               write cmnd[5 + id.len, 99999999] to files(fno), id
               on error server.error = SV$ON.ERROR

               return

               * ======================================================================
               * Write record, retaiining lock
               * In:  fileno (short integer)
               *      id_len (short integer)
               *      id
               *      data

vb.writeu:
               fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
               gosub check.file
               if err then return

               id.len = oconv(cmnd[3,2], 'ISL')
               id = cmnd[5,id.len]

               gosub check.edit   ;* 17 Oct 26 dm, S.64 part 2
               if err then return

               writeu cmnd[5 + id.len, 99999999] to files(fno), id
               on error server.error = SV$ON.ERROR

               return

               * ======================================================================
               * Delete record
               * In:  fileno (short integer)
               *      id

vb.delete:
               fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
               gosub check.file
               if err then return

               id = cmnd[3,99]

               gosub check.edit   ;* 17 Oct 26 dm, S.64 part 2
               if err then return

               delete files(fno), id
               on error server.error = SV$ON.ERROR

               return

               * ======================================================================
               * Delete record, retaining lock
               * In:  fileno (short integer)
               *      id

vb.deleteu:
               fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
               gosub check.file
               if err then return

               id = cmnd[3,99]

               gosub check.edit   ;* 17 Oct 26 dm, S.64 part 2
               if err then return

               deleteu files(fno), id
               on error server.error = SV$ON.ERROR

               return

               * ======================================================================
               * Call catalogued subroutine
               * In:  subr name len (short integer)
               *      subr name (rounded to 2 byte boundary)
               *      arg count (short integer)
               *      arg len (long integer)
               *      arg data (rounded to 2 byte boundary)
               *
               * Out: arg no (short integer)   Always in ascending arg no order
               *      arg len (long integer)
               *      arg data (rounded to 2 byte boundary)

vb.call:
               name.len = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
               * 12 Oct 26 dm  openQM item 241: the lower of 128 and MAXIDLEN (63 unless raised), as in op_jumps.c.  A client
               * (sdclilib.c, sdclient) cannot read MAXIDLEN and keeps 63.
               max.call.len = config('MAXIDLEN')
               if max.call.len > 128 then max.call.len = 128
               if max.call.len < MAX.CALL.NAME.LEN then max.call.len = MAX.CALL.NAME.LEN
               if name.len < 1 or name.len > max.call.len then
                  abort.message = sysmsg(5263) ;* Invalid call name
                  server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                  return
               end

               subr.name = cmnd[3,name.len]
               pos = 3 + name.len + bitand(name.len, 1)

               arg.count = oconv(cmnd[pos,2], 'ISL')
               pos += 2

               * Are we allowed to run this program?

               obj.flags = load.object(subr.name)
               if subr.name[1,1] = '$' or config("SDCLIENT") = 2 or kernel(K$SDNET, -1) then
                  if not(bitand(obj.flags, HDR.SDCALL.ALLOWED)) then
                     abort.message = sysmsg(5264, subr.name) ;* %1 cannot be called from a SDClient session
                     server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                     return
                  end
               end

               if arg.count then
                  mat args = ''
                  for i = 1 to arg.count
                     arg.len = oconv(cmnd[pos,4], 'ILL')
                     pos += 4
                     args(i) = cmnd[pos,arg.len]
                     pos += arg.len + bitand(arg.len, 1)
                  next i

                  callv @subr.name, arg.count, args

                  * Now return values of changed arguments (if any)

                  for i = 1 to arg.count
                     if changed(args(i)) then
                        response := iconv(i, 'ISL') : iconv(len(args(i)), 'ILL') : args(i)
                        if bitand(len(args(i)), 1) then response := ' '
                     end
                  next i
               end else
                  call @subr.name
               end

               gosub reset.environment

               return

               * ======================================================================
               * Execute command
               * In:  Command string
               * Out: Command output

vb.execute:
               if config("SDCLIENT") # 0  or kernel(K$SDNET, -1) then
                  abort.message = sysmsg(5265) ;* Command execution is disabled
                  server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                  return
               end

               execute cmnd capturing response
               st = @system.return.code
               return to return.status.set

               * ======================================================================
               * Response to input
               * In:  Command string
               * Should never happen as client end should detect this

vb.respond:
               server.error = SV$ERROR
               error.msg = sysmsg(5266) ;* Response to prompt sent when no prompt outstanding
               return

               * ======================================================================
               * End command
               * Should never happen as client end should detect this

vb.endcommand:
               server.error = SV$ERROR
               error.msg = sysmsg(5267) ;* End command request sent when no prompt outstanding
               return

               * ======================================================================
               * Network login, request 24 - RETIRED.  The Windows port's, 20 Aug
               * 2026 (its docs/SCRAM_AUTH.md phase 5); W.4 SCRAM phase 5 here,
               * 14 Sep 2026.
               *
               * THE CLEARTEXT LOGIN IS GONE AND THERE IS NO FALLBACK.  This
               * handler read a length-prefixed user name and password out of
               * the packet body and checked them against /etc/shadow through
               * login() - the password crossed the wire in clear.  Requests 47
               * and 48 replace it, and a client that still sends 24 is refused
               * rather than quietly served a weaker path.
               *
               * IT REFUSES HERE RATHER THAN BEING DROPPED FROM THE GATE ABOVE:
               * taking 24 out of that list would answer an old client with 5270
               * "Not logged in", which reads as a client bug rather than as a
               * protocol that moved.
               *
               * THE BODY IS NOT PARSED.  It still carries a password, so nothing
               * here reads it, copies it or lets it reach a variable.
               *
               * LINUX: the APILOGIN=0 branch went with it.  It trusted the Unix
               * socket peer's uid (linuxio.c getpeereid) in place of a password,
               * and its only client, SDConnectUDS, is removed from sdclilib in
               * the same change - the port has neither.
               *
               * The connection is dropped, as it was for a failed login.

vb.login:
               abort.message = sysmsg(5275) ;* Request no longer supported...
               server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
               done = @true

               return

               * ======================================================================
               * Login user for SDLocal connection

vb.local.login:
              * dsm 20240612 openpath @sdsys:@ds:'$LOGINS' to lgn.f else
              * dsm 20240612    abort.message = sysmsg(6050) ;* Cannot open $LOGINS register
              * dsm 20240612    server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
              * dsm 20240612    return
              * dsm 20240612 end

               * mab 20240219  read security.rec from lgn.f, '$SECURE' then
               * mab 20240219     = kernel(K$SECURE, security.rec<1>)
               * mab 20240219  end

               username = logname

               * mab 20240219 if kernel(K$SECURE, -1) then
               * dsm 20240612 readu lgn.rec from lgn.f, username else null
               * 20240219 mab user must be member of sdusers           
               if  not(is_grp_member(username,'sdusers')) then
                     server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                     void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'api refused user=' : username : ' reason=not a member of sdusers (local login)')  ;* 07 Oct 26 PAL-24
                     abort.message = sysmsg(5009) ;* This user is not registered for SD use
                     goto exit.vb.local.login
               end

* 01 Oct 26 dm - S.50, the login hold (see vb.scram.first).
               call !acc_hold('TEST', hold.info, hold.err)
               if hold.err # '' then
                     server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                     void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'api refused user=' : username : ' reason=a backup or restore is running (local login)')  ;* 07 Oct 26 PAL-24
                     abort.message = sysmsg(hold.err<1,1>, hold.err<1,2>, hold.err<1,3>)
                     goto exit.vb.local.login
               end

* 15 Oct 26 dm - INHIBIT.LOGIN (QM4 gaps Q-8): no API session while the administrator has logins inhibited.
               call !login_inhibit('TEST', hold.info, hold.err)
               if hold.err # '' then
                     server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                     void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'api refused user=' : username : ' reason=logins are inhibited (inhibit.login) (local login)')
                     abort.message = sysmsg(hold.err<1,1>, hold.err<1,2>, hold.err<1,3>)
                     goto exit.vb.local.login
               end

               * dsm 20240612 lgn.rec<LGN$DATE> = date()
               * dsm 20240612 lgn.rec<LGN$TIME> = time()
               * dsm 20240612 recordlocku lgn.f, username
               * dsm 20240612 write lgn.rec to lgn.f, username

               logname = username
               * dsm 20240612 i = kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR,lgn.rec<LGN$ADMIN>)
               * mab 20240219    end else      ;* System is insecure
               * mab 20240219      i = kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR,1)    ;* All users are administrators
               * mab 20240219    end

exit.vb.local.login:
               * dsm 20240612close lgn.f
               if server.error = SV$OK then logged.in = @true
               else done = @true

               return

               * ======================================================================
               * SCRAM-SHA-256 login, client-first.  The Windows port's, 19 Aug
               * 2026 (its docs/SCRAM_AUTH.md phase 3); W.4 SCRAM phase 3 here.
               *
               * In:   n,,n=<user>,r=<client nonce>
               * Out:  r=<combined nonce>,s=<salt>,i=<iterations>
               *
               * NO PASSWORD CROSSES THE WIRE IN EITHER DIRECTION.  The server
               * publishes the salt and cost, the client answers a nonce, and a
               * capture yields an offline guess bounded by the iteration count.
               * THE SERVER DOES NOT RUN PBKDF2 HERE: it holds StoredKey, so login
               * costs two HMACs and a hash.
               *
               * THIS PROCESS IS STILL ROOT HERE (sdclient@.service), which is
               * what lets it read $cred, root:root 0700.

vb.scram.first:
               gosub scram.trim.body

               * A fresh exchange on an authenticated session is a client bug or
               * an attempt to change identity part way through.
               if logged.in then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'sequence error - already authenticated'
                  abort.message = sysmsg(5273) ;* Authentication sequence error
                  server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                  goto exit.vb.scram.fail
               end

               scram.stage = 0        ;* a restart abandons any exchange in flight

* 01 Oct 26 dm - S.50, THE LOGIN HOLD: no new API session while BACKUP.ACCOUNT
*   or RESTORE.ACCOUNT runs (owner, 1 Oct 2026; LOGIN refuses the same way).
*   Before any credential is looked at, so a refused client learns nothing else.
               call !acc_hold('TEST', hold.info, hold.err)
               if hold.err # '' then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'a backup or restore is running'
                  abort.message = sysmsg(hold.err<1,1>, hold.err<1,2>, hold.err<1,3>)
                  server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                  goto exit.vb.scram.fail
               end

* 15 Oct 26 dm - INHIBIT.LOGIN (QM4 gaps Q-8), the same refusal, in the same place.
               call !login_inhibit('TEST', hold.info, hold.err)
               if hold.err # '' then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'logins are inhibited (inhibit.login)'
                  abort.message = sysmsg(hold.err<1,1>, hold.err<1,2>, hold.err<1,3>)
                  server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                  goto exit.vb.scram.fail
               end

* 15 Sep 26 dm - S.19: THE GS2 HEADER FOLLOWS THE TRANSPORT; THE CLIENT DOES NOT
*   CHOOSE IT.  Every socket connection is TLS (gplsrc/sd_tlssrv.c) and must
*   bind the login to it with 'p=tls-exporter,,' (RFC 9266).  A session with no
*   TLS - a local pipe - has nothing to bind to and must say 'n,,'.  'y,,' is
*   refused everywhere.  A client that offered 'n,,' over TLS would be a
*   downgrade, so it is a malformed message here, not a weaker login.
               scram.cbind = SDEXT('', @FALSE, SD_TLS_CBIND)
               if scram.cbind = '' then scram.gs2 = 'n,,' else scram.gs2 = 'p=tls-exporter,,'
               if cmnd[1,len(scram.gs2)] # scram.gs2 then goto scram.bad.message

               scram.cfirst.bare = cmnd[len(scram.gs2)+1,99999999]

               * Exactly two attributes: RFC 5802 requires failing on an m=
               * extension, and counting commas makes that automatic.
               if count(scram.cfirst.bare, ',') # 1 then goto scram.bad.message

               scram.attr = field(scram.cfirst.bare, ',', 1)
               if scram.attr[1,2] # 'n=' then goto scram.bad.message
               username = scram.attr[3,99999999]

               * Taken before any check, so every refusal has a name to record.
               gosub scram.clean.name

               scram.attr = field(scram.cfirst.bare, ',', 2)
               if scram.attr[1,2] # 'r=' then goto scram.bad.message
               scram.cnonce = scram.attr[3,99999999]
               if scram.cnonce = '' then goto scram.bad.message

               * No =2C / =3D unescaping is needed: valid_os_name allows no
               * character that would have required it.
               if username = '' or username[1,1] = '$' then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'unusable name'
                  goto scram.bad.cred
               end
               if not(valid_os_name(username)) then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'name rejected by valid_os_name'
                  goto scram.bad.cred
               end

               openpath @sdsys:@ds:'$cred' to scram.cred.f else
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'cannot open $cred'
                  abort.message = sysmsg(5274) ;* Authentication service unavailable
                  server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                  goto exit.vb.scram.fail
               end

               * LINUX: record ids are lower case (plan M), as !CRED_SET writes
               * them; the port reads upcase(username).
               read scram.cred from scram.cred.f, downcase(username) else
                  close scram.cred.f
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'no credential'
                  goto scram.bad.cred      ;* no credential - fail closed
               end
               close scram.cred.f

               if scram.cred<CRED$VERSION> # SCRAM$CRED.VERSION then
                  scram.cred = ''
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'credential is not version 2 - run modify.password'
                  goto scram.bad.cred
               end

               scram.salt = scram.cred<CRED$SALT>
               scram.iter = scram.cred<CRED$ITER>
               scram.storedkey = scram.cred<CRED$STOREDKEY>
               scram.serverkey = scram.cred<CRED$SERVERKEY>
               scram.cred = ''

               if scram.salt = '' or scram.iter = '' then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'credential incomplete'
                  goto scram.bad.cred
               end
               if scram.storedkey = '' or scram.serverkey = '' then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'credential incomplete'
                  goto scram.bad.cred
               end

               * 18 random bytes: a multiple of three, so base64 has no '='
               * padding and every character is inside RFC 5802's printable set.
               scram.snonce = SDEXT('18', @TRUE, SD_RANDBYTES)
               if scram.snonce = '' then goto scram.crypto.failed

               scram.nonce = scram.cnonce : scram.snonce

               * Kept verbatim: server-first goes into AuthMessage byte for byte.
               scram.sfirst = 'r=' : scram.nonce : ',s=' : scram.salt : ',i=' : scram.iter

               scram.user = username
               scram.stage = 1
               response = scram.sfirst

               return

               * ======================================================================
               * SCRAM-SHA-256 login, client-final.  The port's.
               *
               * In:   c=biws,r=<combined nonce>,p=<ClientProof>
               * Out:  v=<ServerSignature>
               *
               * The server never holds ClientKey.  It recovers a candidate from
               * the proof and checks its SHA-256 against StoredKey.

vb.scram.final:
               gosub scram.trim.body

               if logged.in then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'sequence error - already authenticated'
                  abort.message = sysmsg(5273) ;* Authentication sequence error
                  server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                  goto exit.vb.scram.fail
               end

               * A client-final with no client-first has no nonce and no
               * AuthMessage: refusing is what stops a check against empty values.
               if scram.stage # 1 then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'sequence error - no client-first'
                  abort.message = sysmsg(5273) ;* Authentication sequence error
                  server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                  goto exit.vb.scram.fail
               end

               * ONE ATTEMPT PER CLIENT-FIRST, cleared before the proof is read.
               scram.stage = 0

               if count(cmnd, ',') # 2 then goto scram.bad.message

* 15 Sep 26 dm - S.19: c= is base64 of the GS2 header plus, over TLS, this
*   session's binding; 'biws' is base64('n,,').  Recomputed rather than carried
*   from client-first.  The client's copy must equal the server's: a login
*   relayed by a man in the middle carries the relay's binding, not this
*   session's, and the proof - computed over this attribute - fails with it.
               scram.cbind = SDEXT('', @FALSE, SD_TLS_CBIND)
               if scram.cbind = '' then scram.cattr = 'c=biws' else scram.cattr = 'c=' : scram.cbind
               scram.attr = field(cmnd, ',', 1)
               if scram.attr # scram.cattr then goto scram.bad.message

               scram.attr = field(cmnd, ',', 2)
               if scram.attr[1,2] # 'r=' then goto scram.bad.message

               * Exact match on the whole combined nonce: this kills replay.
               if scram.attr[3,99999999] # scram.nonce then goto scram.bad.message

               scram.cfinal.bare = scram.cattr : ',' : scram.attr

               scram.attr = field(cmnd, ',', 3)
               if scram.attr[1,2] # 'p=' then goto scram.bad.message
               scram.proof = scram.attr[3,99999999]
               if scram.proof = '' then goto scram.bad.message

               scram.authmsg = scram.cfirst.bare : ',' : scram.sfirst : ',' : scram.cfinal.bare

               * Every failure from here is one answer.
               scram.csig = SDEXT(scram.storedkey : @FM : scram.authmsg, @TRUE, SD_HMACSHA256)
               if scram.csig = '' then goto scram.crypto.failed

               scram.ckey = SDEXT(scram.proof : @FM : scram.csig, @TRUE, SD_XORBYTES)
               if scram.ckey = '' then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'client key derivation failed'
                  goto scram.bad.cred
               end

               scram.derived = SDEXT(scram.ckey, @TRUE, SD_SHA256)
               scram.ckey = ''
               if scram.derived = '' then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'stored key derivation failed'
                  goto scram.bad.cred
               end

               * Constant time, as !CRED_VERIFY is.
               if SDEXT(scram.derived : @FM : scram.storedkey, @TRUE, SD_CTEQUAL) # '1' then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'wrong password'
                  goto scram.bad.cred
               end

               * AUTHENTICATED.  The reply is a signature only a holder of this
               * account's ServerKey can compute - the mutual half.
               scram.ssig = SDEXT(scram.serverkey : @FM : scram.authmsg, @TRUE, SD_HMACSHA256)
               if scram.ssig = '' then goto scram.crypto.failed

* 14 Sep 26 dm - LINUX: MAY THIS USER USE THE API AT ALL?  After the proof (so
*   the distinct refusal leaks nothing).  THE NAME IS scram.user, the one just
*   proven.  sdusers first, with 5009 - an SD user at all.
               if not(is_grp_member(scram.user, 'sdusers')) then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'not a member of sdusers'
                  abort.message = sysmsg(5009) ;* This user is not registered for SD use
                  server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                  goto exit.vb.scram.fail
               end

* 20 Sep 26 dm - S.29 PART 3: THE sdapi GATE IS BACK, AND IT IS WHAT MAKES THE
*   ROUTE REAL.  S.28 disposed of it; that disposal is reversed by the owner's
*   ruling of 18/19 Sep 2026 (relayed by the port): every account has ssh and
*   the API by default, and the administrator may turn either off and on
*   again.  Parts 1
*   and 2 gave the groups and the words; until this test existed, MODIFY.ACCOUNT
*   <acc> NONE reported the routes gone and the account was still reachable.
*
*   THE SHAPE IS THE PORT'S AND THE NUMBER IS THE PORT'S (10073).  Membership of
*   sdapi IS the route: an ALLOW group, so a missing or unreadable register
*   refuses rather than grants.
*
*   ***status() IS READ AND A THREE-VALUED ANSWER IS NOT COLLAPSED.***
*   !is_grp_member returns @false both for "not a member" and for "could not
*   tell" (is_grp_member:48/:87/:91 set status 1 for an empty argument, an
*   unreadable /etc/group and an unopenable /etc).  Both refuse here - an allow
*   group fails closed in either case - but the two are written into the audit
*   trail as different reasons, because "this account has no API route" and "the
*   machine could not answer" call for different action from whoever reads it.
*
*   SDSYS IS EXEMPT BY NAME, as it is at vb.account's group test above and for
*   the same reason: the sdsys register record's ACC$GROUP is "sdsys", the OS
*   user IS the account, and nothing joins it to sdapi.  Its own door is the
*   next two gates, which are stricter than this one - so the exemption widens
*   nothing.  The name is compared downcased, as everywhere else.
*
* 17 Oct 26 dm - S.65 slice 2c: A SECOND, NARROWER ROUTE - sdfiles.  A user in sdusers who is not in sdapi but is in sdfiles is let in
*   FILES ONLY: api.files.login is set here, the mark (files.only) just before logged.in, and the request loop then allows only what
*   !apifilesok allows.  sdapi WINS when both: a member of sdapi keeps the whole API as before.  The three-valued reading is kept for
*   BOTH groups: could not tell refuses, with its own reason, and sdsys is exempt from both by name (it has the whole API through its
*   own doors below).  A user in neither is refused exactly as before, same reason, same 10073.  Both groups are off for a new account.
               api.files.login = @false
               api.files.write = @false
               if not(downcase(scram.user) = 'sdsys') then
                  api.in.sdapi = is_grp_member(scram.user, 'sdapi')
                  api.route.stat = status()
                  if api.route.stat # 0 then
                     scram.refuse.reason = 'the sdapi route could not be read, status ' : api.route.stat
                     abort.message = sysmsg(10073, scram.user) ;* %1 is not permitted to use the API
                     server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                     goto exit.vb.scram.fail
                  end
                  if not(api.in.sdapi) then
                     api.in.sdfiles = is_grp_member(scram.user, 'sdfiles')
                     api.files.stat = status()
                     if api.files.stat # 0 then
                        scram.refuse.reason = 'the sdfiles route could not be read, status ' : api.files.stat
                        abort.message = sysmsg(10073, scram.user) ;* %1 is not permitted to use the API
                        server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                        goto exit.vb.scram.fail
                     end
                     if not(api.in.sdfiles) then
*                       19 Oct 26 (K3): the decision is made on both groups, so the audit reason names both (it named sdapi only)
                        scram.refuse.reason = 'not a member of sdapi or sdfiles'
                        abort.message = sysmsg(10073, scram.user) ;* %1 is not permitted to use the API
                        server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                        goto exit.vb.scram.fail
                     end
                     api.files.login = @true
*
* 18 Oct 26 dm - S.65 WRITE GROUP.  A files-only login may change state or take a lock only if the user is ALSO in sdfileswrite, which is
*   off for a new account (core direction 1; SDSYS gives it with modify.account files-write-on).  Read three-valued, like sdfiles above: a
*   group file that cannot be read refuses the login with its own reason, because "could not tell" must not become either answer.  A group
*   that does not exist reads as "not a member": no write, the login stands.  sdapi members never get here, so they are untouched.
                     api.files.write = is_grp_member(scram.user, 'sdfileswrite')
                     api.write.stat = status()
                     if api.write.stat # 0 then
                        scram.refuse.reason = 'the sdfileswrite route could not be read, status ' : api.write.stat
                        abort.message = sysmsg(10073, scram.user) ;* %1 is not permitted to use the API
                        server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                        goto exit.vb.scram.fail
                     end
                  end
               end

* 18 Sep 26 dm - TEARDOWN (S.28, was S.17).  THE SDSYS DOOR: SDSYS is refused
*   over the API unless the connection came from this machine - loopback, or
*   the Unix socket (!peer_local).  After the proof, so the distinct refusal
*   leaks nothing to somebody without the password; before the identity is
*   taken, so a refused session never becomes the user.  10174, the port's
*   number, Linux wording.
*
* 19 Sep 26 dm - 10174 IS OURS ALONE NOW.  It was taken FROM the port, but the
*   port DELETED its own 10174 (and 10175) in its tier teardown of 18 Sep 2026
*   - its RELEASE_1.1 64 - so the number is live here and free there, in a
*   space the two ports share.  Its agent flagged it 19 Sep 23:30 and has
*   recorded 10174 as spoken for at our request, so a later Windows session
*   cannot take it and give it a second meaning.  Kept rather than moved: it is
*   in service here and renumbering a shipped refusal costs more than the
*   bookkeeping does.  ***THE GENERAL POINT IS WORTH MORE THAN THIS NUMBER: a
*   deletion in one port is invisible to the other, exactly as an allocation
*   is, so "the port's number" is a fact about the day it was taken and not a
*   standing guarantee.***
*
*   WHY IT MATTERS: SDSYS can change every account on this system, and the API
*   admits a connection on the SD password alone.  On this machine SDSYS is
*   admitted - the port's owner runs a local application that way - and
*   ssh -L is the way in from elsewhere, because the tunnel needs the Linux
*   login first.  The comparison is against the sdsys name itself: there is
*   one administrator now, no register lookup needed.
*
* 19 Sep 26 dm - AND THE ADDRESS WAS NEVER ENOUGH FOR THIS ONE ACCOUNT (owner,
*   19 Sep 2026: only a user actually logged in as sdsys reaches SDSYS; local
*   API access is kept, and a second password is worth it to have it).
*
*   THE FLAW IN THE OLD TEST: !peer_local classifies an ADDRESS, and its own
*   header says it cannot see through an ssh tunnel - "ssh -L" ends on this
*   host, so a tunnelled connection arrives from 127.0.0.1 or from the socket
*   and reads LOCAL.  That was accepted everywhere else because a tunnel needs
*   the account's own Linux login first.  ***THAT ARGUMENT DOES NOT HOLD FOR
*   SDSYS***: sdsys is DenyUsers'd at sshd (ssh-forcecommand.sh), so sdsys can
*   never own a tunnel - a tunnel carrying SDSYS's credential belongs to SOME
*   OTHER USER, and no address can tell.
*
*   WHAT REPLACES IT: system(43), the kernel's own view of the process at the
*   other end of the Unix socket (SO_PEERCRED, linuxio.c, read before the TLS
*   relay takes the descriptor).  Under a tunnel that process is sshd running
*   as the TUNNEL'S OWNER, so it reports that user and never sdsys.
*
*   THREE CONDITIONS, ALL REQUIRED, AND "" IS A REFUSAL NOT A GAP: the peer
*   must be local, the transport must be the Unix socket (TCP has no such
*   credential, so loopback TCP no longer admits SDSYS - a real narrowing,
*   stated rather than glossed), and the socket's peer must BE sdsys.
               api.peer.local = @false
               call !peer_local(api.peer.local, api.peer.addr)
*
*   TWO REFUSALS, NOT ONE, AND 10174 KEEPS ITS OWN CASE.  "You are not on this
*   machine" and "you are on it but you are not sdsys" are different facts and
*   an administrator reading a log needs to know which.  Keeping 10174 on the
*   remote arm also leaves §13e E4 measuring what it has always measured, and
*   leaves no message record without a caller - tonight's other lesson.
               if downcase(scram.user) = 'sdsys' and not(api.peer.local) then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'sdsys on a remote API session from ' : api.peer.addr
                  abort.message = sysmsg(10174)
                  * SDSYS may be reached through the API only from this machine
                  server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                  goto exit.vb.scram.fail
               end
               if downcase(scram.user) = 'sdsys' then
                  api.peer.user = downcase(trim(system(43)))
                  sdsys.api.ok = @true
                  if api.peer.addr[1,1] # '/' then sdsys.api.ok = @false
                  if api.peer.user # 'sdsys' then sdsys.api.ok = @false
                  if not(sdsys.api.ok) then
                     if api.peer.user = '' then
                        scram.refuse.reason = 'sdsys API session with no socket credential from ' : api.peer.addr
                     end else
                        scram.refuse.reason = 'sdsys API session opened by ' : api.peer.user : ' from ' : api.peer.addr
                     end
                     abort.message = sysmsg(10922)
                     * SDSYS may use the API only from a process running as sdsys
                     server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                     goto exit.vb.scram.fail
                  end
               end

               * THE IDENTITY IS THE NAME THAT WAS PROVEN, UNCONDITIONALLY - no
               * APILOGIN branch.  The answer is taken, as the port's is (its
               * PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 99): K$SET.USERNAME reports the name as it
               * stands, so a refused or overlength name shows here.
               logname = scram.user
               set.name = kernel(K$SET.USERNAME, scram.user)
               if set.name # scram.user then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'the session user name could not be set'
                  abort.message = sysmsg(10160)
                  * Authentication succeeded but the session could not take your user name
                  server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                  goto exit.vb.scram.fail
               end

               scram.storedkey = ''
               scram.serverkey = ''

* 14 Sep 26 dm - BECOME THE USER, BEFORE logged.in, WHICH IS THE WHOLE CONTROL.
*   LINUX: K$ASSUME.USER is initgroups/setgid/setuid while still root
*   (op_kernel.c), the drop login_user's password path makes (S.15).  A
*   refusal leaves logged.in false and ends the connection, so the caller has
*   authenticated into nothing rather than into a root session.
               if not(kernel(K$ASSUME.USER, scram.user)) then
                  scram.refuse.reason = 'could not assume the Linux identity of the authenticated user'
                  abort.message = sysmsg(5277) ;* Authentication succeeded but...
                  server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                  goto exit.vb.scram.fail
               end

* 17 Oct 26 dm - S.65 slice 2c: THE FILES-ONLY MARK, set once here, after every refusal and before logged.in, and never cleared.  It is
*   in the common block (see its declaration), so an abort does not lose it.  The login is audited as a login by this route.
               if api.files.login then
                  files.only = @true
                  files.write = api.files.write   ;* 18 Oct 26, S.65 write group: in the common block for the same reason as files.only
                  if files.write then write.word = 'on' else write.word = 'off'
                  void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'api login route=sdfiles write=' : write.word : ' user=' : scram.user)
               end

               response = 'v=' : scram.ssig
               logged.in = @true

               return

               * ----------------------------------------------------------------------
               * Shared exits for both SCRAM handlers, the port's.

scram.bad.message:
               if scram.refuse.reason = '' then scram.refuse.reason = 'malformed authentication message'
               abort.message = sysmsg(5272) ;* Invalid authentication message
               server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
               goto exit.vb.scram.fail

scram.crypto.failed:
               if scram.refuse.reason = '' then scram.refuse.reason = 'authentication service failed'
               abort.message = sysmsg(5274) ;* Authentication service unavailable
               server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
               goto exit.vb.scram.fail

scram.bad.cred:
               * ONE MESSAGE AND ONE DELAY FOR EVERY CREDENTIAL FAILURE.  Known
               * gap, the port's "Still open": an account with no credential
               * fails at 47 and a wrong password at 48, so the round trip tells
               * them apart.
               if scram.refuse.reason = '' then scram.refuse.reason = 'credential'
               abort.message = sysmsg(5017) ;* Invalid username or password
               server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
* 04 Oct 26 dm S.60: THE DELAY IS TAKEN AFTER THE REFUSAL IS AUDITED, NOT BEFORE.
*   It was "sleep 3" here, ahead of exit.vb.scram.fail.  A client that dropped the
*   connection during those three seconds ended this process before the audit
*   line was written, so a refused login left no trail: measured on Debian 13 and
*   Fedora 44, five of five drops at 0.6-2.5 s wrote nothing, a client that waited
*   wrote one.  Anyone guessing passwords over the API could therefore choose not
*   to be recorded.  The delay itself is unchanged and still slows a guesser;
*   exit.vb.scram.fail now writes the line first and then waits.
* 12 Oct 26 dm  The length of the wait is the PWDELAY configuration parameter (openQM item 106),
*   seconds, 3 unless sd.conf says otherwise (openQM's own default is 5); 0 means no wait.
               scram.fail.delay = config('PWDELAY')
               goto exit.vb.scram.fail

exit.vb.scram.fail:
               * THE ONE PLACE A REFUSED SCRAM LOGIN IS RECORDED: every path out
               * of both handlers passes here, so a refusal added later is
               * audited whether or not its author thinks about the trail.
               if scram.refuse.reason # '' then
                  if scram.audit.name = '' then scram.audit.name = '(none)'
                  void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'api refused user=' : scram.audit.name : ' reason=' : scram.refuse.reason)  ;* 07 Oct 26 PAL-24
                  scram.refuse.reason = ''
               end
               * The wait comes AFTER the record, never before it (S.60 above).
               if scram.fail.delay > 0 then
                  sleep scram.fail.delay
                  scram.fail.delay = 0
               end

               * The connection goes, as for a failed request 24.
               scram.stage = 0
               scram.storedkey = ''
               scram.serverkey = ''
               done = @true

               return

               * ----------------------------------------------------------------------
               * Drop trailing NULs from the request body.  The packet carries its
               * own length, but a client written from vb.login's two-byte padding
               * would put one inside the base64; NUL is not valid in SCRAM.

scram.trim.body:
               loop
               while cmnd # '' and cmnd[len(cmnd),1] = char(0)
                  cmnd = cmnd[1,len(cmnd)-1]
               repeat

               return

               * ----------------------------------------------------------------------
               * The presented user name, made safe for the audit trail (the
               * port's).  audit_message() writes verbatim and ends with a newline,
               * so a name carrying one would forge a line.  The permitted set is
               * valid_os_name's own; anything else becomes ?.

scram.clean.name:
               scram.cn.chars = 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789._-'
               scram.cn.raw = username
               scram.cn.trunc = @false
               if len(scram.cn.raw) > MAX.USERNAME.LEN then
                  scram.cn.raw = scram.cn.raw[1, MAX.USERNAME.LEN]
                  scram.cn.trunc = @true
               end

               scram.cn.bad = convert(scram.cn.chars, '', scram.cn.raw)
               if scram.cn.bad # '' then
                  scram.cn.raw = convert(scram.cn.bad, str('?', len(scram.cn.bad)), scram.cn.raw)
               end

               scram.audit.name = scram.cn.raw
               if scram.audit.name = '' then scram.audit.name = '(empty)'
               if scram.cn.trunc then scram.audit.name := '...'
               return

               * ======================================================================
               * Select index
               * In:  file no  (short int)
               *      list no  (short int)
               *      index name len (short int)
               *      index name (padded to 2 byte interval)
               *      index value len (short int)
               *      index value (padded to 2 byte interval)

vb.selectindex:
               fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
               gosub check.file
               if err then return

               listno = oconv(cmnd[3,2], 'ISL')
               gosub check.list
               if err then return

               * Extract index name

               pos = 5
               n = oconv(cmnd[pos,2], 'ISL')
               pos += 2
               index.name = cmnd[pos, n]
               pos += n + bitand(n, 1)

               * Extract index value

               n = oconv(cmnd[pos,2], 'ISL')
               pos += 2
               index.value = cmnd[pos, n]

               selectindex index.name, index.value from files(fno) to listno

               return

                     * ======================================================================
                     * Open file SDNet file
                     * In:  Account name;filename
                     * Out: fileno (short integer)

vb.open.sdnet:
                     if not(bitand(config('NETFILES'), 2)) then
                        set.status ER$XREMOTE
                        server.error = SV$ELSE
                           return
                        end

                        * 18 Oct 26 dm - THE ACCOUNT RULES, asked BEFORE anything is marked or opened.  Bit 2 of NETFILES was the whole
                        *   gate: with it set, any API login opened any account's file.  Now SDCLIENT refuses as it does request 4 (the
                        *   5262 text), and sdnet.account.check asks what request 3 asks (a member of the account's group, the
                        *   account not suspended), that the account has a register record (a path names none), and that its NET gate is
                        *   on.  The default stays as secure as it was: bit 2 is 0, and the request is refused.
                        if config('SDCLIENT') # 0 then
                           abort.message = sysmsg(5262) ;* File access is disabled
                           server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                           return
                        end
                        net.acct = field(cmnd, ';', 1)
                        gosub sdnet.account.check
                        if net.refused then return

                        * Use of this operation identifies the process as a SDNet user.
                        * Set the appropriate process flag so that LISTU etc show this.

                        i = kernel(K$SDNET, 1)

                        * Locate a spare file table entry

                        n = inmat(files)

                        for i = 1 to n
                           if not(fileinfo(files(i), FL$OPEN)) then goto vb.open.sdnet.continue
                        next i

                        * Extend file table

                        dim files(n + 50)
                        i = n + 1

vb.open.sdnet.continue:

                        * Open file to file table entry i

                        acc = net.acc.path          ;* The account's directory, from its register record (sdnet.account.check, above)
                        file = field(cmnd, ';', 1)  ;* File name

                        qvoc.path = acc:@ds:'voc'
                        openpath qvoc.path to qvoc.f else
                           server.error = SV$ELSE
                              return
                           end

                           file = field(cmnd, ';', 2)
                           if file[1,5] = 'DICT ' then
                              j = 3
                              file = file[6,999]
                           end else
                              j = 2
                           end

                           read voc.rec from qvoc.f, file else null
                           close qvoc.f

                           if voc.rec[1,1] # 'F' then
                              set.status ER$VNF
                              server.error = SV$ELSE
                                 return
                              end

                              path = voc.rec<j>
                              if path = '' then
                                 set.status ER$NPN
                                 server.error = SV$ELSE
                                    return
                                 end

                                 if upcase(path[1,6]) = '@SDSYS' then path = @sdsys:path[7,9999]

                                 if path[1,1] # @ds then
                                    * Convert voc item to absolute path
                                    path = acc : @ds : path
                                 end

                                 * 20 Oct 26 dm - THE OS SWITCH, ASKED OF A POINTER THAT LEADS OUT OF THE ACCOUNT (S.63 closed in this request; the owner delegated the
                                 *   choice).  The file is opened below with openpath from an internal program, which the OS gate exempts, so an F-pointer in the account's
                                 *   VOC at a directory outside the account (a path, a relative path that climbs out, a link, another account's directory, a system file)
                                 *   was opened for any caller the NET gate admitted.  DIR, OPENSEQ, the OSCOPY verbs and every OPEN in a program ask the question, and now so
                                 *   does this.  It is asked of the account NAMED, by its register record read a moment ago in sdnet.account.check, not of the account this
                                 *   session is in: the pointer is that account's, and a caller who could LOGTO it and OPEN the name there is held to that account's switch.
                                 *   An account with os on passes, and so does SDSYS in its own account; any other path must be inside the account's directory (K$PATH.IN is
                                 *   the geometry alone, with ".." and links resolved, and a name that merely begins with the account's name is not inside).  _voc_ref lets a
                                 *   restricted account follow the stock VOC's own pointers to system files; a remote caller needs none of those, so they are refused here.
                                 net.free = (upcase(trim(net.rec<ACC$OS.EXEC>)) = 'YES' or (downcase(@logname) = 'sdsys' and net.acct = 'sdsys'))
                                 if not(net.free) then
                                    if not(kernel(K$PATH.IN, acc : @fm : path)) then
                                       void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'api refused request=29 reason=os gate off user=' : @logname : ' account=' : net.acct : ' path=' : path)
                                       set.status ER$PERM
                                       server.error = SV$ELSE
                                       return
                                    end
                                 end

                                 openpath path to files(i)
                                 on error server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                                 then
                                    if bitand(fileinfo(files(i), FL$FLAGS), FL$FLAGS.TRUSTED) then
                                       server.error = SV$ELSE
                                       end else
                                          response = iconv(i, 'ISL')
                                       end
                                    end
                                    else server.error = SV$ELSE

                                       return

                                       * ======================================================================
                                       * Lock record
                                       * In:  fileno (short integer)
                                       *      flags (short integer)    0x0001 = Update lock, 0x0002 = no wait.
                                       *      id

vb.lock.record:
                                       fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
                                       gosub check.file
                                       if err then return

                                       flags = oconv(cmnd[3,2], 'ISL')

                                       id = cmnd[5,99]

                                       begin case
                                          case flags = 0
                                             recordlockl files(fno), id
                                          case flags = 1
                                             recordlocku files(fno), id
                                          case flags = 2
                                             recordlockl files(fno), id locked server.error = SV$LOCKED
                                             case flags = 3
                                                recordlocku files(fno), id locked server.error = SV$LOCKED
                                             end case

                                             return

                                             * ======================================================================
                                             * Clear file
                                             * In:  fileno (short integer)
                                             * Out:

vb.clearfile:
                                             fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
                                             gosub check.file
                                             if err then return

                                             gosub check.edit   ;* 17 Oct 26 dm, S.64 part 2
                                             if err then return

                                             clearfile files(fno)   ;* 17 Oct 26 dm: was files(i), a stale loop variable, which cleared whichever file it happened to index

                                             return

                                             * ======================================================================
                                             * Get file lock
                                             * In:  fileno (short integer)
                                             *      Wait?  (short integer)
                                             * Out:

vb.filelock:
                                             fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
                                             wait = oconv(cmnd[3,2], 'ISL')
                                             gosub check.file
                                             if err then return

                                             if wait then
                                                filelock files(fno)   ;* 19 Oct 26 (K8a): was files(i), the stale loop variable of the last open, as vb.clearfile's was
                                             end else
                                                filelock files(fno) locked server.error = SV$LOCKED
                                                end

                                                return

                                                * ======================================================================
                                                * Release file lock
                                                * In:  fileno (short integer)
                                                * Out:

vb.fileunlock:
                                                fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
                                                gosub check.file
                                                if err then return

                                                fileunlock files(fno)   ;* 19 Oct 26 (K8a): was files(i)

                                                return

                                                * ======================================================================
                                                * Test lock
                                                * In:  fileno (short integer)
                                                *      id

vb.recordlocked:
                                                fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
                                                gosub check.file
                                                if err then return

                                                id = cmnd[3,99]
                                                i = recordlocked(files(fno), id)
                                                st = status()
                                                response = iconv(i, 'ILL');
                                                set.status st

                                                return

                                                * ======================================================================
                                                * Fetch information about indices
                                                * In:  fileno (short integer)

vb.indices1:
                                                fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
                                                gosub check.file
                                                if err then return

                                                response = indices(files(fno))

                                                return

                                                * ======================================================================
                                                * Fetch information about indices
                                                * In:  fileno (short integer)
                                                *      Index name

vb.indices2:
                                                fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
                                                gosub check.file
                                                if err then return

                                                response = indices(files(fno), cmnd[3,99])

                                                return

                                                * ======================================================================
                                                * Select file and return list
                                                * In:  fileno (short integer)

vb.selectlist:
                                                fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
                                                gosub check.file
                                                if err then return

                                                select files(fno) to 11
                                                readlist response from 11 else null

                                                return

                                                * ======================================================================
                                                * Select index, returning indexed values
                                                * In:  fileno (short integer)
                                                *      index name

vb.selectindexv:
                                                fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
                                                gosub check.file
                                                if err then return

                                                index.name = cmnd[3,999]
                                                selectindex cmnd[3,99] from files(fno) to 11
                                                readlist response from 11 else null

                                                return

                                                * ======================================================================
                                                * Select index, returning keys for indexed value
                                                * In:  fileno (short integer)
                                                *      index name len (short integer)
                                                *      index name
                                                *      indexed value

vb.selectindexk:
                                                fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
                                                gosub check.file
                                                if err then return

                                                i = oconv(cmnd[3,2], 'ISL')
                                                index.name = cmnd[5,i]

                                                selectindex index.name, cmnd[5+i,999] from files(fno) to 11
                                                readlist response from 11 else null

                                                return

                                                * ======================================================================
                                                * Perform FILEINFO() action
                                                * In:  fileno (short integer)
                                                *      key (long integer)

vb.fileinfo:
                                                fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
                                                gosub check.file
                                                if err then return

                                                i = oconv(cmnd[3,4], 'ILL')
                                                response = fileinfo(files(fno), i)

                                                return

                                                * ======================================================================
                                                * READV and variants
                                                * In:  fileno (short integer)
                                                *      flags  (short integer) 1 = update lock, 2 = shared lock, 4 = no wait
                                                *      field no (long integer)
                                                *      id
                                                * Out: record data

vb.readv:
                                                fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
                                                gosub check.file
                                                if err then return

                                                flags = oconv(cmnd[3,2], 'ISL')
                                                field_no = oconv(cmnd[5,4], 'ILL')

* 18 Oct 26 dm - S.65 write group: request 41 is allowed to a files-only login for a plain READV (flags 0), but flags 1, 2, 5 and 6 take a record lock, which
*   is a change of state like requests 7 to 10 and 30.  !apifilesok sees only the number, so the flags are asked here, the same refusal, the same audit line.
                                                if files.only and not(files.write) and flags # 0 then
                                                   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'api refused request=41 route=sdfiles write=off user=' : @logname)
                                                   server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                                                   abort.message = sysmsg(11521, 41) ;* This login cannot change or lock files here: ask the administrator for files-write-on (request %1)
                                                   return
                                                end

                                                id = cmnd[9,99]

                                                begin case
                                                   case flags = 0
                                                      readv rec from files(fno), id, field_no
                                                      on error server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                                                      then response = rec
                                                      else server.error = SV$ELSE

                                                      case flags = 1
                                                         readvu rec from files(fno), id, field_no
                                                         on error server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                                                         then response = rec
                                                         else server.error = SV$ELSE

                                                         case flags = 2
                                                            readvl rec from files(fno), id, field_no
                                                            on error server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                                                            then response = rec
                                                            else server.error = SV$ELSE

                                                            case flags = 5
                                                               readvu rec from files(fno), id, field_no
                                                               on error server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                                                               locked server.error = SV$LOCKED
                                                                  then response = rec
                                                                  else server.error = SV$ELSE

                                                                  case flags = 6
                                                                     readvl rec from files(fno), id, field_no
                                                                     on error server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
                                                                     locked server.error = SV$LOCKED
                                                                        then response = rec
                                                                        else server.error = SV$ELSE
                                                                     end case

                                                                     return

                                                                     * ======================================================================
                                                                     * SetLeft
                                                                     * In:  fileno (short integer)
                                                                     *      index name

vb.setleft:
                                                                     fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
                                                                     gosub check.file
                                                                     if err then return

                                                                     setleft cmnd[3,999] from files(fno)

                                                                     return

